# .gitleaksignore — per-line rationale for every exclusion.
#
# jolarca-control — marketplace governance control plane.
# SOC 2 CC6.1 / ISO 27001 A.8.24 / GDPR Art.32.
#
# POLICY: this file must stay as small as possible. Every entry carries a
# dated rationale naming the rule, file and line it suppresses, and why no
# literal credential could ever live there.
#
# ── Current state ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# ZERO entries. The working tree has no literal secret shape at any path:
# the AWS/Stripe synthetic fixtures in `tests/test_repo_readiness_audit.py`
# and `tests/test_first_commit_pipeline.py` are assembled at runtime via
# string concatenation (`"AKIA" + "Q3EG…"`), so no continuous AKIA{16} or
# sk_live_{24,} byte sequence exists in any tracked file. Empirically
# re-verified 2026-10-07 with `gitleaks dir . --config <title-only>` (0 hits)
# and `gitleaks git . --config <title-only>` (0 hits across 86 commits).
#
# Historical blobs at commits 0e17920 / c4b6c5a DO contain literal-shaped
# strings from before the 2026-09-28 refactor, but gitleaks does not flag
# them (its built-in AWS rule requires an accompanying secret-access-key
# or entropy threshold the key-id alone does not meet). The repository's
# own regex sweep in `scripts/repo_readiness_audit.py` DOES flag them as
# L-16 at S2; that residual is documented as an accepted false positive in
# `docs/security/f01-l16-synthetic-fixture-acceptance.md` and no entry is
# needed here because gitleaks is not what fires the finding.
#
# ── Retention rationale ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# Retained as a placeholder so the file exists and its rules are visible:
# if a future contributor introduces a real literal fixture and cannot
# refactor it to runtime assembly, they must add a line here with a dated
# rationale. An empty file plus a documented policy is a deliberate bar --
# adding an exclusion becomes a visible act of acknowledgement in the diff,
# not a silent relaxation of a rule. See AGENTS.md §5 "do not delete or
# weaken a gate to make CI pass".
#
# ── Note on the negative-control claim made by earlier revisions ──────────────
#
# An earlier version of this file's header asserted that
# `tests/test_first_commit_pipeline.py` "fails the build if this file is ever
# emptied". No such test exists in the repository as of 2026-10-07 --
# `grep -Rn gitleaksignore tests/` returns no matching assertions. That was
# a control described as enforced but not present, the exact class AGENTS.md
# §6 and D-40 warn against. Corrected here rather than left in place. If a
# non-emptiness assertion is wanted later, ship the assertion AND the test
# in the same PR; a comment alone is folklore.
